Subject: Cantor Seeded Compiler Slice 7 Lifecycle Validation Protocol Justification

@ [justification_uuid] 37fb8116-5903-4bca-8ca7-ff04017c2b45
@ [source_uuid] 814703e0-0f4f-426a-a37d-60a76c608397
@ [canonical_uuid] 019dcef5-71bd-457a-828b-830a68fae0f1

& [Problem]
  + Slice6 proves a pure native build lifecycle but exposes it only as Rust forms and functions
  + tool callers need a stable machine boundary before Cantor can feed the compiler fabric into inference or other adapters

& [SelectedSolution]
  + implement an owned strict lifecycle request, deterministic staged replay, compact typed response, and provider-free compiler-lab CLI
  + reuse existing validators as authority rather than restating their semantic rules

& [WhyNow]
  + this is the smallest bridge between proven compiler semantics and a usable tool call
  + it creates immediate testability without prematurely granting production build authority

& [RejectedAlternatives]
  + production process runner is rejected because admitted host sandbox and denial evidence remain unresolved
  + MCP and HTTP are deferred because a transport-independent core protocol must be proven first
  + content-addressed handles are deferred because registry custody and availability would add a new trust boundary
  + automatic repair and derivation are rejected because validation must not become hidden authorship

& [Safety]
  + complete supplied forms, public trust material, bounded input, strict schemas, exact replay, no private keys, no runner, and fixed non-authority preserve the Slice6 boundary

& [ExpectedGain]
  + Cantor gains one deterministic callable seam that later tools can invoke and inspect while the effectful compiler remains separately governed
